---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Boot from '../components/Boot.astro';
import '../styles/page.css';

/** Every row here is a real switch in killchain/routing.py. */
const ROUTING = [
  ['DIMENSIONS', 'the refusal dimensions, and the argument for each. Their question text is what Jev is asked.'],
  ['SAFE', 'what a frontier agent builds without comment; the "false" criteria.'],
  ['HARD_RULES', 'regexes that block a send outright, no model involved.'],
  ['FRAMING_HINTS', 'non-blocking warnings for clone, hedge and licence wording.'],
  ['THRESHOLDS', 'risk probability needed to pull a span, per dimension and span kind.'],
  ['classify()', 'the final per-span decision. Any dimension over its bar routes private.'],
  ['pick_frontier()', 'which coding agent builds the public half, by content if you like.'],
  ['split_prompt()', 'how the raw prompt is cut into spans for the risk map.'],
];

const LINKS = [
  { href: 'https://github.com/jcpsimmons/killchain', label: 'github' },
  { href: 'https://pypi.org/project/killchain/', label: 'pypi' },
  { href: 'https://youtube.com/@drjoshcsimmons', label: 'youtube' },
];
---

<Base>
  <div class="page">
    <header class="topbar">
      <span class="topbar__mark"><b>killchain</b></span>
      <span class="topbar__ver">v0.1.0</span>
      <nav class="topbar__nav" aria-label="elsewhere">
        {LINKS.map((l) => <a href={l.href} rel="noopener">{l.label}</a>)}
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main id="main">
      <div class="hero">
        <Boot />
        <div class="hero__under">
          <span>python 3.11+</span>
          <span>· no dependencies</span>
          <span>· MIT</span>
          <span>one prompt in, two halves out</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="wrap">
        <section class="block">
          <div class="slug">
            <span class="slug__n">01</span>
            <span class="slug__t">## what this is</span>
            <span class="slug__rule"></span>
          </div>
          <p class="lede">
            An abliterated orchestrator model is the front door. It is the only model that sees the
            full prompt. It splits the job into a <strong>public half</strong>, built by a frontier
            coding agent, and a <strong>private half</strong>, implemented by the orchestrator
            itself, joined by a contract.
          </p>
          <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
            <p>
              The partition rule is refusal risk, nothing else. Claude and Codex are good and fast,
              but they hedge, caveat, simplify, or refuse when a task looks reverse-engineered,
              unlicensed, regulated, exploitative, or oversized. Those parts go to the uncensored
              model. The frontier gets the rest of the app and never learns how the interesting
              bits work, so it has no position to take.
            </p>
            <p>
              It is not a cost trick and not only a quality trick: a brief that says "match the
              behaviour of X" gets you <span class="cm">from memory</span> disclaimers and invented
              constants. A brief that says "given these fields return a float in [0, 1], monotonic
              in both" gets you working code.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="split">
            <div class="half">
              <h3>public/</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>contracts/ · stubs</li>
                <li>tests/test_contracts.py</li>
                <li>brief, interfaces, decoy fixtures</li>
                <li>NOTES.md</li>
              </ul>
              <span class="tag">built by [CC] or Codex, against stubs</span>
            </div>
            <div class="half half--private">
              <h3>private_impl/</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>raw prompt, contract</li>
                <li>private fixtures</li>
                <li>risk report</li>
                <li>mode 0700, never on the frontier's path</li>
              </ul>
              <span class="tag">implemented by the orchestrator, to the contract</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="slug">
            <span class="slug__n">02</span>
            <span class="slug__t">## install</span>
            <span class="slug__rule"></span>
          </div>
          <p class="prose">
            Python 3.11 or newer, no dependencies. Find out what is missing before you waste a run:
            <code>killchain --doctor</code> checks the config, both API keys, the private directory
            mode, the denylist, and does one live round-trip to each endpoint.
          </p>

          <div class="install">
            <span class="install__label">:!</span>
            <button class="install__copy" type="button" data-copy hidden>copy</button>
            <pre><code><span class="p">$ </span>uv tool install killchain
<span class="p">$ </span>killchain --doctor
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="c">killchain "build X using our proprietary Y"</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>killchain -f prompt.md --frontier codex --yes</code></pre>
          </div>
          <p class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem;opacity:.65;font-size:.9rem">
            <code>pipx install killchain</code>, <code>pip install killchain</code>, or a zero-install
            trial with <code>uvx killchain "your task"</code>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="slug">
            <span class="slug__n">03</span>
            <span class="slug__t">## where to edit the routing</span>
            <span class="slug__rule"></span>
          </div>
          <p class="prose">
            One file decides what the frontier is allowed to see. Everything else is plumbing that
            imports from it. The denylist adds literal and regex rules without touching any code: it
            carries secrets that must never leave, and terms that tip the frontier off.
          </p>

          <div class="table" role="table" aria-label="routing.py surface">
            <div class="tr tr--head" role="row">
              <span role="columnheader">in routing.py</span>
              <span role="columnheader">what it controls</span>
            </div>
            {
              ROUTING.map(([k, v]) => (
                <div class="tr" role="row">
                  <span class="tr__k" role="cell">{k}</span>
                  <span class="tr__v" role="cell">{v}</span>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="slug">
            <span class="slug__n">04</span>
            <span class="slug__t">## trust boundaries</span>
            <span class="slug__rule"></span>
          </div>

          <div class="notes">
            <div class="note">
              <h3>orchestrator</h3>
              <p>
                The full prompt, the contract, and the private implementation loop. This is the half
                that is supposed to know everything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="note">
              <h3>frontier agent</h3>
              <p>
                Only the public contract, the brief, interface signatures and descriptions, and
                decoy fixtures. Told the private functions are a black box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="note">
              <h3>the risk scorer</h3>
              <p>
                The public view span by span, plus raw prompt spans when
                <code>probe_raw_prompt</code> is on. Turn scoring off entirely with
                <code>--no-risk</code>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="prose" style="margin-top:1.8rem">
            Four layers on the outbound view: the orchestrator's partition, a deterministic regex
            gate, a probabilistic risk scorer, and human review of the outbound view before
            anything is sent. Rejections from the gate and the scorer are fed back with the exact
            spans, so the orchestrator re-partitions and re-frames rather than losing the content.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="slug">
            <span class="slug__n">05</span>
            <span class="slug__t">## limits</span>
            <span class="slug__rule"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="notes">
            <div class="note note--warn">
              <h3>it predicts, it does not measure</h3>
              <p>
                The scorer predicts refusal. Refusals are stochastic and version-dependent, and a
                model can still balk at something no dimension predicted.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="note note--warn">
              <h3>v1 scope</h3>
              <p>
                Python interfaces only; the orchestrator writes files by returning JSON, no tool use
                on its side; one contract round, no renegotiation mid-build.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="note note--warn">
              <h3>a convention, not a jail</h3>
              <p>
                The frontier runs with permissions skipped. The private root is never on its cwd or
                its PYTHONPATH, and its environment is scrubbed, but treat that boundary as a
                convention.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </main>

    <footer class="foot">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="foot__row">
          {LINKS.map((l) => <a href={l.href} rel="noopener">{l.label}</a>)}
          <span class="dim">·</span>
          <a href="https://github.com/jcpsimmons/killchain/issues" rel="noopener">issues</a>
          <span class="dim">·</span>
          <span class="dim">MIT</span>
        </div>
        <p class="foot__sig">
          <span class="c">"</span> I keep handing the good model work it will not do. Say which half
          is the risky half once, and each model gets the half it is best suited to.
        </p>
        <p class="foot__sig">— Dr. J</p>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  const btn = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy]');
  const pre = document.querySelector<HTMLPreElement>('.install pre');
  if (btn && pre) {
    btn.hidden = false;
    btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      const text = pre.innerText.replace(/^\$ /gm, '').trim();
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
        btn.textContent = 'copied';
        window.setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = 'copy'), 1600);
      } catch {
        btn.textContent = 'ctrl-c';
      }
    });
  }
</script>
